{"source_id": "W2052218930", "title": "The Pen Is Mightier Than the Keyboard: Advantages of Longhand Over Laptop Note Taking", "year": 2014, "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797614524581", "abstract": "In three experiments, we investigated whether the pen is mightier than the keyboard. In Experiment 1, participants took notes on a lecture using either a laptop or longhand and completed tests of factual and conceptual learning. Longhand note takers performed better on conceptual questions, and laptop note takers wrote more words. In Experiment 2, we examined whether the medium effect persisted when note takers were given time to review their notes before testing; longhand note takers again outperformed laptop note takers on conceptual questions, even after a review period. In Experiment 3, we instructed laptop note takers not to take verbatim notes, which reduced but did not eliminate the longhand advantage."}
</observation>{"thought": "I'll fetch the 2022 medical students study to check whether it addresses review and reports a null medium effect.", "action": "fetch_source", "args": {"source_id": "W4224781631"}}

<observation>
{"source_id": "W4224781631", "title": "No difference in factual or conceptual recall comprehension for tablet, laptop, and handwritten note-taking by medical students in the United States: a survey-based observational study", "year": 2022, "doi": "https://doi.org/10.3352/jeehp.2022.19.8", "abstract": "PURPOSE: Technological advances are changing how students approach learning. The traditional note-taking methods of longhand writing have been supplemented and replaced by tablets, smartphones, and laptop note-taking. It has been theorized that longhand note-taking benefits learning more than laptop note-taking. This survey-based observational study examined note-taking medium (handwritten, tablet, laptop) in relation to factual and conceptual recall comprehension among medical students in the United States. No significant differences were found among the three note-taking mediums for either factual or conceptual recall comprehension. Note review habits were surveyed but did not moderate the relationship between note-taking medium and performance."}
</observation>{"thought": "I have two fetched sources that directly address review as a moderator; I'll finish with specific findings and exact quotes.", "action": "finish", "args": {"findings": [{"claim": "In Experiment 2 of Mueller and Oppenheimer (2014), longhand note takers still outperformed laptop note takers on conceptual questions even when given time to review their notes before testing.", "source_id": "W2052218930", "quote": "In Experiment 2, we examined whether the medium effect persisted when note takers were given time to review their notes before testing; longhand note takers again outperformed laptop note takers on conceptual questions, even after a review period.", "stance": "supports"}, {"claim": "In a survey-based observational study of US medical students, no significant differences were found among handwritten, tablet, and laptop note-taking for factual or conceptual recall, and note review habits did not moderate the medium-performance relationship.", "source_id": "W4224781631", "quote": "Note review habits were surveyed but did not moderate the relationship between note-taking medium and performance.", "stance": "supports"}]}}
